#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179484 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a82102-6dd6-5a73-8370-9050e0f2323a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15149" :question/problem "![](images/05/05c91d681a0c84bbe9d8b6cab9bd8cdf1dca8bf88c851b8c7a7045f73ff9844c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3,$ and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540276 :question-generator/id #uuid "2559158e-ccf7-5367-8a90-3d49434ee990" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186179646.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320053d8-7061-5bee-a125-4b5572fa81bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260325} {:answer/id #uuid "7754550d-7915-5725-8462-298bbf3140d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260917} {:answer/id #uuid "89563c80-e409-593a-b874-aab987bd64d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261072} {:answer/id #uuid "906a6921-6dc7-5a65-9058-0fd796a13a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261132} {:answer/id #uuid "cd431924-213d-5018-895a-46685548488d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "906a6921-6dc7-5a65-9058-0fd796a13a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261132} :answer-field/id #uuid "001f66bd-2162-519e-af79-734f33a25866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea876586-e09c-5050-847d-bc8b312ea302" :question/math-academy-id "q-126881" :question/problem "![](images/19/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=2\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc34a4ce-679b-5695-9dfe-540cd60117ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177922} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fed570-9a0a-5fb6-b092-18090d3757e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177923} {:answer/id #uuid "034cd8e5-64a2-50b6-bec2-58e1146cb7e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177924} {:answer/id #uuid "64ad22d1-d74c-5598-8e39-3026099a604c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177925} {:answer/id #uuid "73d700d6-ddb9-5085-a028-fa38b1441baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177926}] :answer-field/id #uuid "268bf099-9758-5e4a-bc30-751c0618a551" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177921}] :question/id #uuid "30107d9c-6bc8-5ab5-a34a-0024b1e426f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127199" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c8aef7238da3bb5aed0fd2a458f6fd9bcd42884bbd350616e58e7a25b1f0fe2.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F =-2xi - 2yj$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18620d11-ce60-57c2-a580-d44a5579200c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260090} {:answer/id #uuid "3218e783-1e46-5bd3-bd29-5548bb105ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260326} {:answer/id #uuid "76437869-a2cd-55ab-8ebf-6ce70a057af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260908} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4d8c81-c3e3-5854-a2b5-53f7aae6f342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261523} {:answer/id #uuid "e35d583f-1e94-5ce0-ace0-3d795dc7095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18620d11-ce60-57c2-a580-d44a5579200c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260090} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cb0a17-877b-5728-bbcf-86808ed0219e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ae07821-3e5f-5e7b-b438-d7c5726b59a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126890" :question/problem "![](images/70/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b4648b-d247-5f92-9d2b-f244cac28ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259878} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1a741e-d70d-5584-8f47-17f6fe12b753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260521} {:answer/i